\meta{pdf}{http://ozark.hendrix.edu/~yorgey/pub/type-matrices.pdf}
\meta{slides}{http://ozark.hendrix.edu/~yorgey/pub/type-matrices-mpc-15.pdf}

\p{We show that every regular language, via some DFA which accepts it, gives rise to a homomorphism from the semiring of polynomial functors to the semiring of #{n \times n} matrices over polynomial functors. Given some polynomial functor and a regular language, this homomorphism can be used to automatically derive a functor whose values have the same shape as those of the original functor, but whose sequences of leaf types correspond to strings in the language.}

\p{The primary interest of this result lies in the fact that certain regular languages correspond to previously studied derivative-like operations on polynomial functors, which have proven useful in program construction. For example, the regular language #{a^* h a^*} yields the \em{derivative} of a polynomial functor, and #{b^* h a^*} its \em{dissection}. Using our framework, we are able to unify and lend new perspective on this previous work. For example, it turns out that dissection of polynomial functors corresponds to taking \em{divided differences} of real or complex functions, and, guided by this parallel, we show how to generalize binary dissection to #{n}-ary dissection.}
